    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The Gospel of John presents Jesus using powerful symbolic titles that reveal His identity and mission. In this topic, we explore three of these: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">the Lamb of God</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">the Door</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">the Good Shepherd</span>. Each image conveys a different aspect of what Jesus came to do for humanity.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Jesus as the Lamb of God (John 1:29, 36)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When John the Baptist saw Jesus coming toward him, he declared: <em>\"Behold the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!\"</em> (John 1:29). This title connects Jesus to the Old Testament sacrificial system — lambs were offered to atone for sin. Jesus is the perfect, final sacrifice whose blood cleanses humanity from all sin (Isaiah 53:7; 1 Peter 1:19).</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The Passover lamb's blood protected Israel from the angel of death (Exodus 12).</li>\n  <li>Jesus, as the Lamb, offers permanent redemption — not temporary atonement.</li>\n  <li>John's declaration identified Jesus as the fulfilment of all Old Testament sacrifices.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Jesus as the Door (John 10:7–9)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Jesus said: <em>\"I am the door of the sheep. All who came before me are thieves and robbers, but the sheep did not listen to them. I am the door; if anyone enters through me, he will be saved.\"</em> As the door to the sheepfold, Jesus is:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The <strong>only way of access</strong> to God (John 14:6).</li>\n  <li>The source of <strong>salvation</strong> — to enter through him is to be saved.</li>\n  <li>The gateway to <strong>abundant life</strong> — \"he will go in and out and find pasture.\"</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Jesus as the Good Shepherd (John 10:11–15)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Jesus said: <em>\"I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep.\"</em> Unlike a hired hand who flees when danger comes, the Good Shepherd:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Knows His sheep</strong> personally (John 10:14).</li>\n  <li><strong>Lays down His life</strong> willingly for the sheep — a reference to the crucifixion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Protects and provides</strong> for the sheep at all costs.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Memory:</strong> John 1:29 — Lamb; John 10:7–9 — Door; John 10:11–15 — Good Shepherd. All three are found in the Gospel of John.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus is the Lamb who takes away sin (John 1:29), the Door who is the only way to salvation (John 10:7–9), and the Good Shepherd who lays down His life for His sheep (John 10:11–15). These images reveal His role as Saviour, Mediator, and Protector.\n</div>\n  ",
