    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic deepens our understanding of the three images — Lamb, Door, and Good Shepherd — with further teachings and their practical application to the life of a believer. We examine what it means to live under these realities in today's world.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Further Teachings on Jesus as the Lamb</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The Lamb imagery extends through Scripture. In Revelation 5, the Lamb is 'standing as if slain' — bearing the marks of sacrifice yet alive and victorious. Jesus the Lamb is not a defeated victim but a triumphant Saviour. Believers are called to live in the reality of His redemption — free from sin's power and guilt.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Redemption applied:</strong> By faith in the Lamb, sin is forgiven (1 John 1:7).</li>\n  <li><strong>Ongoing intercession:</strong> Jesus as the Lamb intercedes for believers (Romans 8:34).</li>\n  <li><strong>Communion:</strong> The Lord's Supper commemorates the Lamb's sacrifice (1 Corinthians 11:26).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Further Teachings on Jesus as the Door</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">There is only one door — no other religion, philosophy, or system leads to God. Jesus's claim is exclusive: <em>'I am the way and the truth and the life. No one comes to the Father except through me'</em> (John 14:6). Application for believers:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Enter through the Door by <strong>repentance and faith</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Once inside, enjoy <strong>security</strong> — no one can snatch you from the Father's hand (John 10:28–29).</li>\n  <li>The Door is open to <strong>all people</strong> — no qualification required except trust in Jesus.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Further Teachings on the Good Shepherd</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Psalm 23 celebrates God as the Shepherd who provides, leads, and comforts. Jesus fulfils this image. Practical lessons for today:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Jesus searches for the lost:</strong> The parable of the lost sheep (Luke 15:4–7) shows the shepherd leaves 99 to find 1.</li>\n  <li><strong>Spiritual nourishment:</strong> The Shepherd leads to 'green pastures' — Scripture, prayer, and fellowship.</li>\n  <li><strong>Protection from wolves:</strong> False teachers are described as wolves (Matthew 7:15); the Shepherd guards against them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Eternal security:</strong> John 10:28 — 'I give them eternal life, and they will never perish.'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Application:</strong> Living under the Lamb: confess sin and receive forgiveness. Living in the Door: enter by faith, remain secure. Living under the Shepherd: follow His voice (Scripture), resist false teachers.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus the Lamb offers redemption and intercedes for us (Romans 8:34). Jesus the Door provides exclusive salvation and security (John 10:28–29). Jesus the Shepherd searches for the lost (Luke 15), provides, protects, and gives eternal life (John 10:28). Psalm 23 is the OT background for the Shepherd image.\n</div>\n  ",
