    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  In John 15:1–11, Jesus delivers one of His most intimate teachings, declaring: <em>'I am the true vine.'</em> This teaching was given in the Upper Room on the night of His betrayal. It describes the essential relationship between Jesus and His followers, and reveals what it means to live a fruitful Christian life.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Vineyard Image (John 15:1–2)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Jesus says: <em>'I am the true vine, and my Father is the gardener. He cuts off every branch in me that bears no fruit, while every branch that does bear fruit he prunes so that it will be even more fruitful.'</em></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>The Vine:</strong> Jesus — the source of all spiritual life and nourishment.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Gardener:</strong> God the Father — who cultivates, disciplines, and tends.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Branches:</strong> Believers — who draw life from the vine.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Importance of Abiding (John 15:4–5)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'Remain in me, as I also remain in you. No branch can bear fruit by itself; it must remain in the vine. Neither can you bear fruit unless you remain in me.'</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>'Abide' (remain/stay)</strong> means maintaining a living, dependent relationship with Jesus through prayer, Scripture, obedience, and fellowship. The branch cannot produce fruit independently — all life flows from the vine.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Consequences of Abiding and Not Abiding</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Abiding in Christ</th><th>Not Abiding</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Bears much fruit (v.5)</td><td>Bears no fruit (v.4)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prayers answered (v.7)</td><td>Thrown away and withers (v.6)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Joy made full (v.11)</td><td>Burned (v.6)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Glorifies the Father (v.8)</td><td>Proves nothing</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The 'True' Vine</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In the Old Testament, Israel was called God's vine (Isaiah 5:1–7; Psalm 80:8), but it failed to bear fruit. Jesus is the 'true' vine — the perfect, faithful Israel — who produces real spiritual fruit and through whom alone genuine fruit is possible.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Verse:</strong> John 15:5 — 'I am the vine; you are the branches. If you remain in me and I in you, you will bear much fruit; apart from me you can do nothing.'\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus is the True Vine; believers are branches; God the Father is the gardener (John 15:1). Abiding in Christ means depending on Him for spiritual life — through prayer, Scripture, and obedience. Fruit = good works, Christlike character. Without abiding, there is no fruit. Israel was God's vine but failed; Jesus is the true, faithful vine.\n</div>\n  ",
