    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  In John 6:25–40 and John 4:7–15, Jesus declares Himself the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Bread of Life</span> and the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Living Water</span>. Both images address the deepest human hungers and thirsts — spiritual needs that only Jesus can satisfy.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Jesus as the Bread of Life (John 6:25–40)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">After feeding 5,000 people with five loaves and two fish, the crowd followed Jesus. He challenged them: <em>'You are looking for me, not because you saw the signs but because you ate the loaves and had your fill.'</em> (John 6:26). He then declared: <em>'I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to me will never go hungry, and whoever believes in me will never be thirsty.'</em> (John 6:35)</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Context:</strong> The crowd wanted physical bread; Jesus pointed to spiritual bread.</li>\n  <li><strong>Background:</strong> Israel received manna in the wilderness (Exodus 16), but it was temporary. Jesus is the true, living bread from heaven that gives eternal life.</li>\n  <li><strong>The promise (v.37):</strong> 'All that the Father gives me will come to me, and whoever comes to me I will never drive away.'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Jesus as the Living Water (John 4:7–15)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">At Jacob's Well, Jesus spoke to a Samaritan woman and said: <em>'Everyone who drinks this water will be thirsty again, but whoever drinks the water I give them will never thirst. Indeed, the water I give them will become in them a spring of water welling up to eternal life.'</em> (John 4:13–14)</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The Samaritan woman was seeking physical water; Jesus offered spiritual satisfaction.</li>\n  <li>Living water = the Holy Spirit (John 7:37–39).</li>\n  <li>A 'spring welling up' = not just received once, but continually flowing from within.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comparison</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Physical Bread/Water</th><th>Jesus as Bread/Water</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Satisfies temporarily</td><td>Satisfies permanently — 'never hunger/thirst'</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Must be eaten/drunk repeatedly</td><td>Received once by faith; eternally effective</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sustains physical life</td><td>Sustains and gives eternal spiritual life</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Verses:</strong> John 6:35 — 'I am the bread of life'; John 4:14 — 'a spring of water welling up to eternal life'; John 7:37–39 — living water = the Holy Spirit.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus as Living Bread (John 6) promises that those who come to Him will never hunger spiritually. As Living Water (John 4), He promises that those who drink will never thirst again — the water becomes an internal spring. Both images contrast earthly, temporary satisfaction with Jesus's eternal, complete fulfilment.\n</div>\n  ",
