    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">1 John 4:7–12</span> teaches that love among believers is not optional — it is the essential mark of authentic Christianity. Because God is love and has loved us, we are commanded and enabled to love one another. The love of God is made visible in the world through how Christians love each other.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Source of Love (1 John 4:7–8)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'Dear friends, let us love one another, for love comes from God. Everyone who loves has been born of God and knows God. Whoever does not love does not know God, because God is love.'</em></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Love originates in <strong>God</strong>, not in human nature.</li>\n  <li>Loving others is evidence of being <strong>born again</strong> and <strong>knowing God</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Not loving others is a sign of not truly knowing God.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Demonstration of God's Love (1 John 4:9–10)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'This is how God showed his love among us: He sent his one and only Son into the world that we might live through him. This is love: not that we loved God, but that he loved us and sent his Son as an atoning sacrifice for our sins.'</em></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>God took the initiative — love originates with God, not us.</li>\n  <li>The cross is the supreme historical proof of God's love.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Command to Love (1 John 4:11–12)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'Dear friends, since God so loved us, we also ought to love one another. No one has ever seen God; but if we love one another, God lives in us and his love is made complete in us.'</em></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Our love for each other makes God's invisible presence <strong>visible</strong> in the world.</li>\n  <li>Jesus said: 'By this everyone will know that you are my disciples, if you love one another' (John 13:35).</li>\n  <li>Christian community is meant to be a demonstration of God's love to the watching world.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. How to Love One Another</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Forgiveness (Ephesians 4:32)</li>\n  <li>Encouragement (Hebrews 10:24–25)</li>\n  <li>Serving one another (Galatians 5:13)</li>\n  <li>Speaking truth in love (Ephesians 4:15)</li>\n  <li>Bearing one another's burdens (Galatians 6:2)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Verse:</strong> 1 John 4:11 — 'Since God so loved us, we also ought to love one another.' And John 13:35 — 'By this everyone will know that you are my disciples, if you love one another.'\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Love among believers flows from God's nature (God is love — 1 John 4:8). God demonstrated love first (sent His Son — v.9–10). Because He loved us, we must love each other (v.11). Mutual love makes God's invisible presence visible (v.12). Love = the defining mark of a Christian disciple (John 13:35).\n</div>\n  ",
