    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Justification by faith</span> is a central doctrine of Christianity, especially emphasised in Paul's letters. It means that a person is declared righteous before God not by keeping the law or performing good works, but through <span class=\"learn-keyword\">faith in Jesus Christ</span>. Key passages are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Romans 5:1–5</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Galatians 2:16</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. What Justification Means</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Justification</strong> is a legal term: God the Judge declares the guilty person 'not guilty' — righteous — on the basis of Christ's righteousness imputed (credited) to the believer. It is 'just-as-if-I'd never sinned.'</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Declared righteous</strong> — not made morally perfect immediately, but declared righteous in God's court.</li>\n  <li><strong>By grace</strong> — unmerited, unearned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Through faith</strong> — the instrument by which we receive justification.</li>\n  <li><strong>In Christ</strong> — because of His life, death, and resurrection.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Galatians 2:16 — Not by the Law</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'Know that a person is not justified by the works of the law, but by faith in Jesus Christ... because by the works of the law no one will be justified.'</em> Paul's point: no amount of law-keeping or religious effort can make a person right with God. Justification is received, not achieved.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Romans 5:1–5 — Benefits of Justification</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'Therefore, since we have been justified through faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom we have gained access by faith into this grace in which we now stand.'</em></p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Peace with God</td><td>Romans 5:1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Access into grace</td><td>Romans 5:2</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hope of God's glory</td><td>Romans 5:2</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rejoicing in sufferings</td><td>Romans 5:3</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>God's love poured out by the Spirit</td><td>Romans 5:5</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Distinction:</strong> Justification = God's declaration about our status (we are righteous). Sanctification = God's ongoing work making us righteous in character. Justification is instantaneous; sanctification is a lifelong process.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Justification = declared righteous by God through faith in Christ (Romans 5:1; Galatians 2:16). Not by law-keeping or good works. Benefits: peace with God, access to grace, hope of glory, power in suffering, the Spirit's love. Abraham's example: 'Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him as righteousness' (Romans 4:3).\n</div>\n  ",
