    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">2 Corinthians 5:17</span> declares: <em>'If anyone is in Christ, the new creation has come: the old has gone, the new is here!'</em> And <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Romans 6:1–11</span> explains that through union with Christ in His death and resurrection, believers are dead to sin and alive to God. New life in Christ is both a fact and a call.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. New Creation (2 Corinthians 5:17)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Being 'in Christ' (Greek: en Christo) is Paul's shorthand for the entire Christian life. The person who is in Christ is a <span class=\"learn-keyword\">new creation</span>:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The <strong>old</strong> (old nature, old standing before God, old way of life) <strong>has gone</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The <strong>new</strong> (new identity, new relationship with God, new moral direction) <strong>has come</strong>.</li>\n  <li>This is not self-improvement but a radical, divine transformation — a new creation (like Genesis 1).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Death and Resurrection with Christ (Romans 6:1–11)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Paul asks: 'Shall we go on sinning so that grace may increase?' His answer: 'By no means!' The reason: through baptism (a symbol of union with Christ), believers have died to the old life and been raised to new life:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Death to sin (v.6):</strong> 'Our old self was crucified with him so that the body ruled by sin might be done away with.'</li>\n  <li><strong>Alive to God (v.11):</strong> 'Count yourselves dead to sin but alive to God in Christ Jesus.'</li>\n  <li><strong>Practical implication (v.12–13):</strong> Do not let sin reign in your body; offer yourselves to God as instruments of righteousness.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Characteristics of New Life</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>New desires — loving what God loves</li>\n  <li>New direction — pursuing righteousness</li>\n  <li>New power — the Holy Spirit</li>\n  <li>New identity — child of God</li>\n  <li>New relationships — loving community</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Contrast:</strong> Romans 6:23 — 'The wages of sin is death, but the gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord.' Old life = sin and death. New life = righteousness and eternal life.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> New creation in Christ = old gone, new come (2 Corinthians 5:17). Union with Christ means dying to sin and living to God (Romans 6:1–11). Baptism symbolises this death and resurrection. Christians are to 'count' themselves dead to sin and alive to God — and live accordingly.\n</div>\n  ",
