    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Matthew 18:21–35</span> (the Parable of the Unmerciful Servant) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Luke 17:3–4</span> teach that Christians are commanded to forgive others as God has forgiven them. Forgiveness is not optional — it flows from the grace already received.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Standard of Forgiveness (Matthew 18:21–22)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Peter asked: 'Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother or sister who sins against me? Up to seven times?' Jesus answered: 'Not seven times, but seventy-seven times' (or seventy times seven — Matthew 18:22). This means <strong>unlimited forgiveness</strong> — not literally counting to 490, but forgiving without keeping score.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Parable of the Unmerciful Servant (Matthew 18:23–35)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>A king forgave a servant an enormous debt — 10,000 talents (millions).</li>\n  <li>The same servant refused to forgive a fellow servant a tiny debt — 100 denarii.</li>\n  <li>The king was furious: 'I cancelled all that debt of yours because you begged me to. Shouldn't you have had mercy on your fellow servant just as I had on you?'</li>\n  <li>The servant was thrown into prison.</li>\n  <li>Lesson: God's forgiveness of our sin is so immeasurably greater than any wrong done to us that withholding forgiveness is an outrage.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Condition of Forgiveness</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Matthew 6:14–15: <em>'If you forgive other people when they sin against you, your heavenly Father will also forgive you. But if you do not forgive others their sins, your Father will not forgive your sins.'</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Luke 17:3–4 shows forgiveness is to be offered repeatedly — even seven times a day, if the person repents and asks.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Forgiveness does not mean pretending the wrong did not happen, enabling further abuse, or that trust is automatically restored. It means releasing the offender from a debt, freeing yourself from bitterness, and leaving justice to God.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Forgiveness standard: unlimited (Matthew 18:22). Basis: the immeasurable debt God forgave us (Matthew 18:23–35). Condition: Matthew 6:14–15 — forgiving others is connected to receiving God's forgiveness. Luke 17:3–4 — repeated forgiveness. Colossians 3:13 — 'Forgive as the Lord forgave you.'\n</div>\n  ",
