    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Philippians 2:1–11</span> presents Jesus Christ as the supreme model of humility — the eternal Son of God who humbled Himself to become human and die on the cross. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Matthew 18:1–4</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">James 4:10</span> reinforce that humility is the mark of greatness in God's kingdom.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Model of Humility — Christ (Philippians 2:5–11)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'In your relationships with one another, have the same mindset as Christ Jesus: Who, being in very nature God, did not consider equality with God something to be used to his own advantage; rather, he made himself nothing by taking the very nature of a servant, being made in human likeness. And being found in appearance as a man, he humbled himself by becoming obedient to death — even death on a cross!'</em></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Jesus did not exploit His divine status but <strong>emptied Himself</strong> (kenosis).</li>\n  <li>He took the form of a <strong>servant</strong> (doulos = slave).</li>\n  <li>He was obedient even to <strong>death on the cross</strong> — the most humiliating death.</li>\n  <li>Result: God exalted Him — 'every knee shall bow' (v.9–11).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Call to Humility (Philippians 2:3–4)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'Do nothing out of selfish ambition or vain conceit. Rather, in humility value others above yourselves, not looking to your own interests but each of you to the interests of the others.'</em></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Humility and Greatness (Matthew 18:1–4)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The disciples asked: 'Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?' Jesus called a child and said: 'Whoever humbles himself like this little child is the greatest in the kingdom.' God's kingdom reverses human values — the humble are the greatest.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Promise to the Humble</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>James 4:10 — 'Humble yourselves before the Lord, and he will lift you up.'</li>\n  <li>1 Peter 5:6 — 'Humble yourselves, therefore, under God's mighty hand, that he may lift you up in due time.'</li>\n  <li>Proverbs 11:2 — 'With humility comes wisdom.'</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>True Humility:</strong> Not low self-esteem or self-deprecation — but an accurate view of oneself before God (Romans 12:3: 'Do not think of yourself more highly than you ought') and a genuine concern for others' interests.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Christ's humility (Philippians 2:5–11) is the supreme model: He emptied Himself, took servant form, and died on the cross. The humble are greatest in God's kingdom (Matthew 18:4). Humility before God leads to exaltation (James 4:10; 1 Peter 5:6). Pride leads to a fall (Proverbs 16:18).\n</div>\n  ",
