    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">James 2:1–13</span> directly addresses the sin of showing favouritism in the church — treating the rich better than the poor. God is impartial (Acts 10:34; Romans 2:11), and His people are called to reflect this impartiality in all relationships.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Problem: Favouritism (James 2:1–4)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">James gives a vivid scenario: a wealthy man in fine clothes and a poor man in dirty clothes both enter the assembly. If you give the rich man the best seat and tell the poor man to sit on the floor, you have 'discriminated among yourselves and become judges with evil thoughts.'</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. God's Standard: No Partiality (Acts 10:34; Romans 2:11)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Peter declared: 'I now realise how true it is that God does not show favouritism but accepts from every nation the one who fears him and does what is right.' (Acts 10:34–35). Romans 2:11: 'God does not show favouritism.'</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Royal Law (James 2:8)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'If you really keep the royal law found in Scripture, \"Love your neighbour as yourself,\" you are doing right.'</em> (Leviticus 19:18; Matthew 22:39) Impartiality is the application of this royal law — loving all neighbours equally, regardless of status.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Consequences of Partiality (James 2:9–13)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Showing favouritism is sin (v.9).</li>\n  <li>Breaking one command makes one guilty of all (v.10–11 — the law is a unity).</li>\n  <li>Judgment is without mercy to those who have shown no mercy (v.13).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Verse:</strong> James 2:9 — 'But if you show favouritism, you sin and are convicted by the law as lawbreakers.'\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Favouritism (partiality) is sin (James 2:9). God shows no partiality (Acts 10:34; Romans 2:11). All people bear God's image and deserve equal dignity. The royal law (love your neighbour) requires impartial love. In Nigeria, impartiality means treating all tribes, social classes, and genders with equal respect.\n</div>\n  ",
