    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">sovereignty of God</span> means that God is in ultimate control of all things — history, nations, nature, and individual lives. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Daniel 4:34–35</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Romans 8:28</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Isaiah 46:9–10</span> establish that God reigns supreme over all creation and His purposes cannot be thwarted.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. What God's Sovereignty Means</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Sovereignty means God is:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Omnipotent</strong> — all-powerful; nothing is too hard for Him (Jeremiah 32:17).</li>\n  <li><strong>Omniscient</strong> — all-knowing; He knows the end from the beginning (Isaiah 46:10).</li>\n  <li><strong>Omnipresent</strong> — everywhere present; nothing escapes His knowledge (Psalm 139).</li>\n  <li><strong>Supreme over all</strong> — kings, nations, and history are under His rule (Daniel 2:21).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Nebuchadnezzar's Testimony (Daniel 4:34–35)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">After God humbled the proud king, Nebuchadnezzar declared: <em>'His dominion is an eternal dominion; his kingdom endures from generation to generation. All the peoples of the earth are regarded as nothing. He does as he pleases with the powers of heaven and the peoples of the earth. No one can hold back his hand or say to him: \"What have you done?\"'</em></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Sovereignty and Human Responsibility</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">God's sovereignty does not eliminate human responsibility. Both are true:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Joseph's brothers sold him (their evil act) — yet God meant it for good (Genesis 50:20).</li>\n  <li>Christ was crucified by wicked hands (human responsibility) — yet by God's set purpose and foreknowledge (Acts 2:23).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Romans 8:28 — Sovereignty and Suffering</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>'And we know that in all things God works for the good of those who love him, who have been called according to his purpose.'</em></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>'All things' — not just good things; includes pain, loss, and injustice.</li>\n  <li>'Works for the good' — God weaves all things into His redemptive purpose.</li>\n  <li>'Those who love him' — the promise is for believers, not a general law.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Key Truth:</strong> God's sovereignty is not fatalism (all events are mechanical and impersonal) — it is a personal, wise, loving oversight of all things by a God who can be trusted.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> God is sovereign over all creation (Daniel 4:34–35). Isaiah 46:9–10: God declares the end from the beginning. Romans 8:28: God works all things for the good of those who love Him. Joseph's and Jesus's stories show sovereignty working through and despite human sin. Human responsibility and divine sovereignty coexist.\n</div>\n  ",
